Image: [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remote_work_Telelavoro_1.jpg)

This is a short article with reflections on so-called "Hybrid" jobs
and the need to establish a proper definition to avoid confusion.

## On-site Work

First we have on-site work, where you are obliged to go to an office
or specific place to do your duties. The 2020 pandemic showed a
shift towards remote. The first years of the 2020s proved that remote
work is possible for a large part of tech jobs.

## Hybrid Work

After the pandemic, so-called "hybrid" jobs started to appear. There
is a clear misreading of what Hybrid work is. One might assume "some
days remote and some days on site", but that causes confusion.

The definition I propose for hybrid is the following:

- You work on a specific schedule and time zone.
- An office is available but attending is *optional*.
- You are obliged to stay within a territory (for example within
  Chile, for legal, tax and other reasons).

In simple terms: if you are forced to go on site, then it is an
on-site job, not a hybrid one.

## Remote Work

Remote work is similar to hybrid with the caveat that it can be
anywhere in the world or time zone — you are not obliged to be in
the same country where the job is.

It is more intended for overseas job offers where you pay your own
social security, health insurance and transfer costs.

## Why does it matter?

Many people say they like going to the office for various reasons.
Working remotely is often confused with working from home. Which is
wrong. Working remotely simply means you have the freedom to work
wherever is most comfortable. It can even be an office, but the key
is the freedom to choose where you work. This is why many defend
going to an office and prefer it over the remote option. The drama
is that going to the office should not be mandatory in a hybrid job
— it is optional, and there should be no problem if you want to
work outside of it all the time. The office becomes an optional
coworking space rather than a place of submission and slavery to
schedules and chair-warming expectations.

Think of all the hours of life you lose just by going somewhere
mandatory: mental health costs, food and transportation costs.
Costs you personally assume, not the company.

Let your employment relationship be of mutual benefit and not
one-sided for the company.

As technology professionals it is in our power to demand this
benefit from companies. If we submit to forced on-site attendance,
we hurt both our mental health and our loved ones, and above all we
hurt the industry as a whole, making working conditions more boring
for every member of the trade.

## Conclusion

Prioritize your life and your mental and financial health: always
prefer jobs where going on site is optional.
